;;; A rule's concluded `neq` is stored as a fact — M1f **W30**, register row
;;; `W30/assert-neq-w-x` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked by S1f.5.0
;;; (2026-11-08) from the inventory of 2026-11-06's G-21 (its probe
;;; `assert_neq.ein`, the program unchanged), filed `open` and owned by
;;; S1f.5.7, on the user's ruling of 2026-11-07 (Q-M1e2.26, G-21).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact `(p a)` and a rule concluding `(neq w ?x)` from `(p ?x)`. `w` is
;;; a name of the program (a rule's `:assert` names it), so `(p w)` is a
;;; candidate.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `eq` and `neq` are relations whose facts are fixed by name identity,
;;; computed and never stored, and a rule's conclusion of one is checked: a
;;; true one adds nothing, a false one makes its state a contradiction
;;; (G-21). At root `(neq w a)` holds and adds nothing. At `{(p w)}` the rule
;;; concludes `(neq w w)`, which is false, so that state is dead. R is {∅},
;;; one state, root's `(p a)` alone.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The conclusion is stored: root holds `(neq w a :rule r)` (`ein saturate
;;; FILE --dump`), and `{(p w)}` holds `(neq w a)` and the false `(neq w w)`,
;;; so R is two states, exit 0 (`ein solve FILE`, its `--dump-answer` state
;;; files, and `ein oracle FILE` at `ec004a48`, the engine `916d97ac`'s,
;;; release and debug alike). Neither state file loads: a fact line
;;; `(neq w a)` is a parse error, so `answer_round_trip.rs` and
;;; `answer_dump.rs` name this file until S1f.5.7 fixes W30.

(relation p Node)
(p a)
(rule r () :match (p ?x) :assert (neq w ?x))
